{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-104151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{3} & =0 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f74073ef60c16018a62a03d492dd9b603e902501267462d9009fe87e46bf806"} {"question" "q-104151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(u+v)^{3}"] ["math" "(u-v)^{3}"] ["math" "({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})^{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x+y}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1-x-y}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{4-x-y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\frac{4-x-y}{3}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\frac{4-u-v}{3}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{4-u-v}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f74073ef60c16018a62a03d492dd9b603e902501267462d9009fe87e46bf806"} {"question" "q-104179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2+u-v}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2-u-v}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2-u-v}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{4-u-v}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4-u-v}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4-u-v}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0$\nfor $z\\ge 0.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4{r}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{4{r}^{2}} \\\\ z & =2r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f74073ef60c16018a62a03d492dd9b603e902501267462d9009fe87e46bf806"} {"question" "q-104290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2r"] ["math" "4{r}^{2}"] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2r"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f74073ef60c16018a62a03d492dd9b603e902501267462d9009fe87e46bf806"} {"question" "q-104368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{ _____ },\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f74073ef60c16018a62a03d492dd9b603e902501267462d9009fe87e46bf806"} {"question" "q-104378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"} {"question" "q-104378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070325/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8eb79f45854c047f1a1085606d9da74b9f859a3d68b4059f75e571bdfa91a1a"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2489 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
